WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.557
Book Three, Proposition Five.

2
00:00:02.557 --> 00:00:07.493
If two circles cut one another, they will not have the same center.

3
00:00:07.493 --> 00:00:12.650
Let the circles A B C and C D G cut one another at the points B and C.

4
00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:16.644
I say that they will not have the same center.

5
00:00:16.644 --> 00:00:27.520
For, if possible, let their common center be E; join E C, and draw E F G through at random, meeting circle A B C at F and circle C D G at G.

6
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:36.073
Since the point E is the center of the circle A B C, the radius E C equals E F by Definition Fifteen.

7
00:00:36.073 --> 00:00:42.192
Again, since E is the center of the circle C D G, the radius E C equals E G.

8
00:00:42.192 --> 00:00:51.291
Because E C equals both E F and E G, E F is also equal to E G, the less equal to the greater—which is impossible.

9
00:00:51.291 --> 00:00:57.008
Therefore the point E is not the center of the circles A B C and C D G.

10
00:00:57.008 --> 00:00:58.860
Which was to be proved.

11
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:17.615
As Heath remarks, Propositions Three Five and Three Six express a single geometric principle: whether two distinct circles cut one another or touch one another, if their circumferences share even one point, they cannot share a center; conversely, concentric circles with a common circumference point coincide completely.

12
00:01:17.615 --> 00:01:31.480
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred two rests directly on the definitions of circle and center, requiring only twelve ancestor nodes while supporting thirty-four downstream theorems across Books Three, Four, Six, Twelve, and Thirteen.

